The problem with simply buying puts on $UVXY is that you need a very large decline to make serious money. When the $VIX falls, the implied volatility of $UVXY options tends to fall as well, reducing the value of your puts. So you’re fighting two forces: the decline in the underlying helps your position, but the decline in implied volatility hurts it. Unless $UVXY falls sharply enough, you can be right about the direction and still make very little money.

@Jarrettbinx You are correct; this is why position size is your only risk management tool

The problem with shorting VIX futures is that you eventually have to roll them, and the longer-dated futures won’t give you the same bang for your buck.

In contrast, instruments like $VXX and $UVXY are structurally designed to lose value over time, particularly when the futures curve is in contango.

You want to bet against the house over time, not just for a day, a week, or a month. These instruments allow you to do exactly that.

